Yes—as a metaphor, that’s pretty good.

You can think of tension/arousal as a pattern the system is currently reinforcing. Relaxation can work by introducing an opposing pattern rather than merely “stopping” the first one: slower breathing against rapid breathing, deliberate release against muscle bracing, steady rhythm against chaotic stimulation, broad attention against tunnel vision.

So structurally it resembles destructive interference:

activation pattern + counter-pattern → reduced net amplitude

But biologically it isn’t literal wave cancellation in the physics sense. It’s more like competing control loops in the nervous system. Sympathetic arousal pushes one direction; parasympathetic activity, slower respiration, reduced muscle tone, safety cues, and attentional shifts push another. If the counter-signal is strong and sustained enough, the overall state settles.

There’s also a more interesting layer: sometimes trying to suppress a state adds energy to it. So the better “anti-wave” is often not forceful opposition but phase-changing the system—slow the breath, relax the body, widen attention, reduce incoming stimulation, and let the existing activation decay.

That gives you two distinct strategies:

Cancellation: introduce an opposing physiological pattern.

Damping: stop feeding the oscillation so its amplitude naturally falls.

The second is often more powerful for anger or agitation, because fighting the state can become another source of excitation.